-- title:
--   Label unit at an end of the blown-up supersingular chart
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q\ge 5$, a non-zero natural number $M'$ with $q\nmid M'$, and a prime $\ell\ge 3$ with $\ell\neq q$ and $\ell\nmid M'$. Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero, $\xi\in L$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity, and assume (`hι`) that there is a ring homomorphism $\iota:L\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell))$. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L\subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ which (`hK`) is [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79), i.e. the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion function field of $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$, $H$ being the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell)^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell)^\times$. Let $A$ be a henselian discrete valuation ring with fraction field $L$ and algebraically closed residue field, with $q\in\mathfrak m_A$ (`hAq`) and $\xi$ in the image of $A$ (`hξA`), acting on $K$ compatibly with $L$. Let $j\in K$, non-zero, whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81), the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant; let $\varpi$ generate $\mathfrak m_A$, and let $\varpi_t\in A$ satisfy $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1}=q\cdot u$ for some unit $u$ (`hϖt`). Throughout, $C:=$ `chartAlgFin A ↥K j` denotes the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ of elements integral over $A[j]$, and `jChartFin` is $j$ regarded as an element of $C$.
--
--   A supersingular closed point is fixed: a maximal ideal $y$ of $C$ containing the image of $\varpi$ (`hϖy`), with the property (`hss`) that for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi:C\to\Omega$ with kernel $y$ one has $\varphi(j)\in$ [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7), that is, every elliptic Weierstrass curve over $\Omega$ with $j$-invariant $\varphi(j)$ has no non-zero point annihilated by $q$.
--
--   The hypothesis `hArig` (a local-rigidity hypothesis, summarised here) asserts, for every point $z$ of the two-chart integral model [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel A ↥K j`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L236) (the pushout of the two chart spectra $\operatorname{Spec}C$ and $\operatorname{Spec}$ `chartAlgInf`) at which the germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ coming from the structure morphism `toBase` to $\operatorname{Spec}A$ lies in the maximal ideal of the stalk, and for every point $y'$ of `XFin` $=\operatorname{Spec}C$ mapping to $z$ under `ιFin` whose ideal is supersingular in the sense of `hss`, the existence of a complete discrete valuation ring $W$, a homomorphism $\sigma:A\to W$ carrying $\varpi$ to a uniformiser, power series $f,u,v\in W[[X_0,X_1]]$ with $u,v$ units and $f-(X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1)\in(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$, and a ring isomorphism $e$ from the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ onto $S:=W[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma(\varpi_t^{q+1})v-fu)$, subject to seven clauses: $e$ carries the germ of $a\in A$ to the constant $\sigma(a)$; the chart algebra $C$ is stable under every automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ over $L$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$; for $\gamma$ moreover in $\Gamma(\ell)$ such a $\tau$ acts trivially on $C/y'$; for $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ acting trivially on $C/y'$ the induced automorphism of the completed stalk is described by an automorphism $\theta$ of $S$ fixing constants, acting on $X_0,X_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^2$ by a matrix $M$ over $W$, together with a scalar $c$ with $c^{q+1}\equiv1$, $M\equiv c\,\gamma$ modulo $\mathfrak m_W$, $c\equiv1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell)$, and $c\not\equiv1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; primes of $S$ containing $\sigma\varpi$, not containing both $X_0,X_1$, and having tangent directions $(a_1,b_1)$, $(a_2,b_2)$ with $q\nmid a_1b_2-a_2b_1$ have distinct contractions to the stalk; for a prime $P$ of $S$ of tangent direction $(1,0)$ containing $\sigma\varpi$, the germ of $a\in C$ lies in the contraction of $P$ if and only if every Laurent coefficient of $a$ lies in $\mathfrak m_A$; and [`ModularCurve.jqNModC L (q*ℓ)`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) lies in $C$, its difference from a suitable $a_0\in A$ lies in $y'$ and is carried by $e$ to the class of a series $h\in(X_0,X_1)^{e_0}$, $e_0\ge1$, whose degree-$e_0$ form is a unit at every pair $(a,b)\in W^2$ not both in $\mathfrak m_W$ with $a^qb-ab^q\in\mathfrak m_W$.
--
--   The same data are then fixed concretely: a point $z$ of the model with germ $\varpi_z$ of $\varpi$ in the maximal ideal of the stalk; a point $y'$ of `XFin` with `ιFin` sending $y'$ to $z$, satisfying the supersingularity hypothesis `hss'` and with $y'$ the point $y$ (`hy'y`); a complete discrete valuation ring $W_1$ with $\sigma_1:A\to W_1$ carrying $\varpi$ to a uniformiser (`hσ₁`), series $f_1,u_1,v_1$ with $u_1,v_1$ units and $f_1$ congruent to the Drinfeld form $X_0X_1^q-X_0^qX_1$ modulo $(X_0,X_1)^{q+2}$ (`hf₁`), and an isomorphism $e_1$ of the adic completion of the stalk at $z$ with $S=W_1[[X_0,X_1]]/(\sigma_1(\varpi_t^{q+1})v_1-f_1u_1)$; the hypothesis `hW₁` asserts for these data the seven clauses just listed.
--
--   Further data and hypotheses: an ideal $J$ of $C$ which (`hJ`) is the infimum of all ideals of the form $\tau^{-1}$ of the contraction, along $C\to$ stalk $\to$ completion $\xrightarrow{e_1}S$, of the ideal generated by the classes of $\sigma_1\varpi_t$, $X_0$ and $X_1$, as $\gamma$ runs over $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ over the $C$-stable automorphisms of $K$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` at $\gamma^{-1}$; an $A$-subalgebra $B$ of $K$ which (`hB`) is generated over $C$ by the elements $x\in K$ with $x\varpi_t\in J$; a valuation subring $W$ of $K$ containing $B$ (`hBW`); the hypothesis `hR1`, that $C\subseteq B$ and that $K$ is the field of fractions of $B$; the hypothesis `hR2`, that $B$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A$ and that $B/\varpi B$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; the hypothesis `hR3` (five clauses), that the elements of $L$ lying in $W$ are exactly those of $A$, that $\mathfrak m_W$ is generated by $\varpi$, that $W$ is a discrete valuation ring, that an element of $C$ lies in $y$ precisely when it lies in $\mathfrak m_W$, and that $W$ is the localisation of $B$ at $\mathfrak m_W$; and the hypothesis `hEQ` (five clauses, summarised here), that for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure on the residue field $k$ of $A$ there is a surjection $\rho:B\to$ [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q k`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) with kernel $B\cap\mathfrak m_W$, compatible with $A\to k$, and equivariant for the level automorphisms attached to $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W$ through an element $(\mathrm{red}_q\gamma,c)$ of [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) acting by `hAction`, with $c\neq1$ when $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)$ and $\tau\neq\mathrm{id}$; that $B$ is stable under all these automorphisms; that every prime of $B$ containing $\varpi$ is obtained by transporting $B\cap\mathfrak m_W$ by some automorphism attached to $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$; that an element of $B$ all of whose such transports lie in $\mathfrak m_W$ is divisible by $\varpi$; and that an automorphism attached to $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $y$ on $C$ preserves $W$.
--
--   Finally an end of the blow-up is fixed: a subring $O$ of $K$ such that (`hO`) for some non-zero $a\in J$, writing $B_a$ for the $A$-algebra generated over $C$ by the $x\in K$ with $xa\in J$, there is a maximal ideal $P$ of $B_a$ with $O$ the localisation of $B_a$ at $P$, every element of $y$ lying in $O$ a non-unit of $O$, and $B\not\subseteq O$. It is assumed that $O\subseteq W$ (`hOW`), that $O$ is local (`hOloc`) and Noetherian (`hOnoe`), and that $C\subseteq O$ (`hCO`). The last hypothesis `hIgStab` states that every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ whose elements of $L$ are exactly those of $A$, with $\varpi$ in $\mathfrak m_V$, containing $C$, and possessing an element $c\in C$ such that $p(c)$ is a unit of $V$ for every monic $p\in A[X]$, is preserved by every automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ over $L$ satisfying `IsLevelAutAt` at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of an element $u$ of $C$, non-zero in $K$, with the following four properties.
--
--   First, $u$ divides a power of the uniformiser: there are a natural number $k$ and an element of $C$ whose product with $u$ is the image of $\varpi^k$ in $C$.
--
--   Second, $u$ is invariant: for every $\gamma\in\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ and every automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ over $L$ satisfying [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt L (q*ℓ) ξ (q*ℓ) ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (levelH (q*ℓ) M') γ⁻¹ K τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29), one has $\tau u=u$.
--
--   Third, $u$ is a unit at the Igusa-type valuations through the end: for every valuation subring $V$ of $K$ such that the elements of $L$ lying in $V$ are exactly those coming from $A$, such that $\varpi$ lies in $\mathfrak m_V$ whenever it lies in $V$, and such that, assuming $j\in V$, the value $p(j)$ is a unit of $V$ for every monic $p\in A[X]$ with $p(j)\in V$, if moreover $O\subseteq V$ then $u$, when it lies in $V$, is not in $\mathfrak m_V$.
--
--   Fourth, $u$ lies in the maximal ideal at the remaining Igusa-type valuations: under the same hypotheses on $V$, if instead some element of $O$ fails to lie in $V$, then $u$, when it lies in $V$, belongs to $\mathfrak m_V$.
--
--   This is the modular-unit ingredient in the study of the special fibre of the integral model of the modular curve at a supersingular point: it produces a vertical element of the $j$-finite chart algebra, invariant under the level automorphisms attached to $\Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, whose unit locus among the Igusa-type valuation rings of $K$ is exactly the set of those dominating the given end $O$ of the blow-up along the weighted centre $J$; the classical model is Ogg's unit $q^{12}\Delta(qz)/\Delta(z)$ on $X_0(q)$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.exists_verticalUnit_chartAlgFin_mem_maximalIdeal_iff_of_end_blowupChart`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.AuxLevel.exists_verticalUnit_chartAlgFin_mem_maximalIdeal_iff_of_end_blowupChart).
